cards:[
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "1 ▶ Primitives vs objects — and what assignment actually does.",
  "a": "7 primitives are values (compared by value); objects (arrays, functions included) are references. Assignment never copies — it points two names at one object. (Part 2, Idea 1)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "2 ▶ let vs const vs var — scoping and hoisting.",
  "a": "var: function-scoped, hoisted as undefined. let/const: block-scoped with TDZ; const binds the name, does not freeze the object. (Part 2, Idea 1)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "3 ▶ == vs === and the coercion rule.",
  "a": "=== checks type and value; == coerces. The non-issue rule: convert explicitly (String/Number/Boolean) at boundaries, use === everywhere else. (Part 3.2)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "4 ▶ The falsy list — and the classic plain-error.",
  "a": "0, ‘’, null, undefined, NaN, false. [] and {} are TRUTHY — use arr.length, not arr, to test for items. (Part 3.1)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "5 ▶ Closures — what they capture and why they exist.",
  "a": "A function keeps access to the scope where it was defined, even after the outer function returns. Captures variables (not values) — late-binding footgun with loops. (Parts 2, 4.3)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "6 ▶ The prototype chain, concretely.",
  "a": "Property lookup: the object, then its prototype, up to Object.prototype, then null — delegation, not copying. class is sugar over constructor + prototype. (Part 5.2)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "7 ▶ `this` — who decides it?",
  "a": "The CALL: obj.method() binds obj; a detached reference loses it (undefined in strict). Arrows inherit `this` lexically; bind/call/apply override. (Part 2, Idea 4)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 1 - EXPECTED OF EVERYONE",
  "q": "8 ▶ The event loop in one breath.",
  "a": "One thread. Sync code runs, then ALL microtasks (promises), then one macrotask (timer/I/O/UI), then microtasks again. Promise callbacks beat setTimeout — always. (Part 2, Idea 5)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 2 - DIFFERENTIATORS",
  "q": "9 ▶ map/filter/reduce — the three shapes of data work.",
  "a": "map = 1:1 transform, filter = subset, reduce = fold to any value. Any pipeline names itself as one of the three — and they never mutate. (Part 6.1)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 2 - DIFFERENTIATORS",
  "q": "10 ▶ Mutating vs non-mutating array methods.",
  "a": "Mutate: push, sort, reverse, splice. Return new: map, filter, slice, spread. Mixing the two is THE classic array bug. (Part 6.2)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 2 - DIFFERENTIATORS",
  "q": "11 ▶ Spread and immutability.",
  "a": "const next = [...arr, item] / {...obj, x:1} — new reference, original untouched. Shallow: nested objects still shared; use structuredClone for depth. (Part 6.2)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 2 - DIFFERENTIATORS",
  "q": "12 ▶ Why async exists — the single thread.",
  "a": "One call stack; I/O must not block it. Callbacks came first, then promises (composable, no pyramid), then async/await (synchronous-looking). (Part 7.1)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 2 - DIFFERENTIATORS",
  "q": "13 ▶ The sequential-await mistake.",
  "a": "await in a loop serialises independent work. Start together, await together: Promise.all(ids.map(fetch)). Add timeouts via AbortController. (Part 7.3)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 2 - DIFFERENTIATORS",
  "q": "14 ▶ Why frameworks exist.",
  "a": "State ↔ DOM sync is the hard part; frameworks make it explicit (virtual DOM / reactive signals) instead of manual string updates. Delegation + render(state) is the vanilla version. (Part 8.3)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 3 - SENIOR SIGNAL",
  "q": "15 ▶ How V8 runs your code.",
  "a": "Parse → AST → bytecode, then TurboFan JIT-compiles hot paths to machine code; hidden classes + inline caches speed property access. (Part 11.1)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 3 - SENIOR SIGNAL",
  "q": "16 ▶ Memory — and the two classic leaks.",
  "a": "GC marks/sweeps the heap. Leaks: detached DOM nodes holding subtrees via listeners, and closures retaining large scopes. Heap snapshots find both. (Part 11.2)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 3 - SENIOR SIGNAL",
  "q": "17 ▶ Microtasks vs macrotasks, precisely.",
  "a": "After every macrotask, the microtask queue drains completely. A microtask scheduling another microtask starves timers. queueMicrotask runs before any next timer. (Part 11.3)"
 },
 {
  "t": "TIER 3 - SENIOR SIGNAL",
  "q": "18 ▶ When is JavaScript the wrong choice?",
  "a": "CPU-bound work the browser cannot offload, hard real-time, huge in-process compute. In the browser there is no alternative; on the server, pick the right tool. (Part 13)"
 }
],
